Sub-pixel stabilisation while the camera turns (issue #2 follow-up).

VDP1 takes whole-pixel corners and the game rounds each corner on its own,
so corners kick by about a pixel relative to their neighbours every game
frame: distant blocks shake and seams open and close, and upscaling
multiplies it. While the camera turns, all static corners move by one
shared homography, which fits the game's corners to within that rounding.
geometry_stabilize fits it robustly per game frame (direct matches only,
80% of corners must agree within 1.6 px) and gives each agreeing corner a
smooth sub-pixel path: it starts where its path ended and ends at the
homography's image of that start, pulled toward the game's corner and kept
within a pixel of it. The renderer keeps the float end corners and replays
the last presentation of a game frame too while stabilising. Standing,
walking and anything that does not fit are unchanged.

On recorded turns, relative corner kicks of small and mid-size faces drop
from 0.8 to 0.25 px (median) and from 1.5 to 0.7 px (90th percentile).
tests/geometry_interp.c: turn_stabilisation.

